window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-36"]=[{"id":"q4_cont_127","q":"4:127","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:127 · Justice toward orphan women","source":"Cairo I:566–568","cairo":"I:566–568","source_page_first":566,"source_page_last":568,"ar":"وَيَسْتَفْتُونَكَ فِي النِّساءِ قُلِ اللَّهُ يُفْتِيكُمْ فِيهِنَّ وَما يُتْلى عَلَيْكُمْ فِي الْكِتابِ فِي يَتامَى النِّساءِ اللاَّتِي لا تُؤْتُونَهُنَّ ما كُتِبَ لَهُنَّ وَتَرْغَبُونَ أَنْ تَنْكِحُوهُنَّ وَالْمُسْتَضْعَفِينَ مِنَ الْوِلْدانِ وَأَنْ تَقُومُوا لِلْيَتامى بِالْقِسْطِ وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِهِ عَلِيماً (127)\nقلت: و (ما يتلى) : عطف على (الله) ، أي: يفتيكم الله، والمتلو عليكم في الكتاب، أي: في القرآن. وَتَرْغَبُونَ أَنْ تَنْكِحُوهُنَّ حذف الجار، وهو في أو عن، ليصدق النهي بالراغب فيها إذا كانت جميلة، والراغب عنها إذا كانت دميمة، والْمُسْتَضْعَفِينَ عطف على (يتامى النساء) أي: والذي يتلى في المستضعفين من الولدان، وهو قوله تعالى: يُوصِيكُمُ اللَّهُ ... الخ، أو على الضمير في (فيهن) أي: يفتيكم فيهن وفي المستضعفين، و (أن تقوموا) عطف على (المستضعفين) ، أو منصوب بمحذوف، أي: ويأمركم أن تقوموا ... الخ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَيَسْتَفْتُونَكَ يا محمد فِي شأن النِّساءِ من الميراث وغيره، قُلِ اللَّهُ يُفْتِيكُمْ فِيهِنَّ، فيأمركم أن تعطوهن حقهن من الميراث، وَيفتيكم أيضًا فيهن ما يُتْلى عَلَيْكُمْ فِي الْكِتابِ في أول السورة إذ قال: لِلرِّجالِ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا اكْتَسَبُوا وَلِلنِّساءِ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا اكْتَسَبْنَ ثم بيَّنه في تقسيم الميراث في يُوصِيكُمُ اللَّهُ فِي أَوْلادِكُمْ، وقال في اليتامى: وَآتُوا الْيَتامى أَمْوالَهُمْ وَإِنْ خِفْتُمْ أَلَّا تُقْسِطُوا فِي الْيَتامى ... الآية، فقد أفتاكم في اليتامى اللَّاتِي لا تُؤْتُونَهُنَّ ما كُتِبَ لَهُنَّ من الصداق وَتَرْغَبُونَ أَنْ تَنْكِحُوهُنَّ بدون صداق مثلهن، فأمركم أن تنكحوا غيرهن، ولا تنكحوهن إلا أن تقسطوا لهن في الصداق، إذا كانت جميلة، أو لها مال، أو ترغبون عن نكاحهن إذا كانت دميمة، فتعضلوهن لترثوهن، فلا تفعلوا ذلك، بل تزوجوها أو زوجوها، وكانوا في الجاهلية، إذا كانت اليتيمة ذات مال وجمال، رغبوا فيها وتزوجوها بدون صداقها، وإن كانت دميمة ولا مال لها رغبوا عنها وعضلوها، أو زوجوها غيرهم. فنهى الله تعالى الفريقين معا.\nوَيفتيكم أيضًا في الْمُسْتَضْعَفِينَ مِنَ الْوِلْدانِ وهم الصغار، أن تعطوهم حقهم من الميراث مع الكبار، وكانوا لا يورثونهم، رُوِي أن عُيينة بن حصن أتى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال: أُخبرنا أنك تورث النساء والصبيان، وإنما كنا نورث من يشهد القتال ويحوز الغنيمة؟ فقال له صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «كذا أُمِرتُ» ، فنزلت الآية.\nوَيفتيكم أيضًا ويأمركم أَنْ تَقُومُوا لِلْيَتامى بِالْقِسْطِ أي: العدل. وهو خطاب للأئمة أن ينظروا لهم بالمصلحة ويستوثقوا حقوقهم، ويحتاطوا لهم في أمورهم كلها. ثم وعدهم بالثواب على ذلك فقال: وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِهِ عَلِيماً، فيجازيكم على قدر إحسانكم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: يستفتونك عن نساء العلوم الرسمية، وعن يتامى العلوم القلبية، وهن نتائج الأفكار، وهي العلوم اللدنية، والأسرار الربانية التي هي من علوم الحقيقة، ولا تليق إلا بالمستضعفين عند الخليقة، وفى الخبر: «ألا أُخبِركُم بأهل الجَنَّة؟ هو كل ضَعيفٍ متضعَّف، لو أقسمَ على اللهِ لأبرَّهُ في قَسمه» . أو كما قال صلّى الله عليه وسلم. قل الله يفتيكم فيهن فيأمركم أن تأخذوا من العلوم الرسمية ما تتقنون به عبادة ربكم، وترغبوا في علم الطريقة، التي هي علم\nالقلوب، ما تحققون به عبوديتكم، ومن نتائج الأفكار ما تُشاهدون به عظمة ربكم، ويأمركم أن تقوموا بالعدل فى جميع شئونكم، فتعطوا الشريعة حقها والطريقة حقها، وتحفظوا أسرار الحقيقة عن غير مستحقها، والله لا يضيع أجر المحسنين.\nثم أمر بالصلح بين الزوجين عند خوف النشوز، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَيَسْتَفْتُونَكَ فِي النِّساءِ قُلِ اللَّهُ يُفْتِيكُمْ فِيهِنَّ وَما يُتْلى عَلَيْكُمْ فِي الْكِتابِ فِي يَتامَى النِّساءِ اللاَّتِي لا تُؤْتُونَهُنَّ ما كُتِبَ لَهُنَّ وَتَرْغَبُونَ أَنْ تَنْكِحُوهُنَّ وَالْمُسْتَضْعَفِينَ مِنَ الْوِلْدانِ وَأَنْ تَقُومُوا لِلْيَتامى بِالْقِسْطِ وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِهِ عَلِيماً (127)","quran_en":"They seek your legal ruling concerning women. Say: God gives you a ruling about them, and also what is recited to you in the Book concerning orphan girls to whom you do not give what is prescribed for them and whom you desire—or refuse—to marry, and the helpless children. You are commanded to deal justly with orphans. Whatever good you do, God knows it.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: “What is recited to you” (<em>mā yutlā<\/em>) is coordinated with “God”: God gives the ruling, as does what is recited in the Book. The preposition governing <em>targhabūna an tankiḥūhunna<\/em> (“you desire to marry them”) has been omitted and can be either <em>fī<\/em> or <em>ʿan<\/em>, so that it condemns both wishing to marry an attractive orphan at an unfair dower and declining to marry an unattractive one while obstructing her marriage. “The helpless children” can be coordinated with “orphan girls” in the preceding reference, pointing to “God instructs you concerning your children” (4:11), or to the pronoun in “concerning them.” “That you act justly” can be coordinated with the same object or depend upon an omitted expression meaning “and He commands you to deal justly.”<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “What is recited to you” ( mā yutlā ) is coordinated with “God”: God gives the ruling, as does what is recited in the Book. The preposition governing targhabūna an tankiḥūhunna (“you desire to marry them”) has been omitted and can be either fī or ʿan , so that it condemns both wishing to marry an attractive orphan at an unfair dower and declining to marry an unattractive one while obstructing her marriage. “The helpless children” can be coordinated with “orphan girls” in the preceding reference, pointing to “God instructs you concerning your children” (4:11), or to the pronoun in “concerning them.” “That you act justly” can be coordinated with the same object or depend upon an omitted expression meaning “and He commands you to deal justly.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>People ask the Prophet for rulings on women's inheritance and other rights. God commands granting them their rightful shares, already specified earlier in the sūrah, including provisions on the wealth of orphans, marriage of wards and children's inheritance. Guardians must not withhold the dower prescribed for orphan girls and then marry them for less than the dower customarily owing to their peers. If the guardian wishes to marry a beautiful, wealthy orphan, justice requires giving her a suitable dower, otherwise marrying someone else. If he refuses to marry a girl because she is considered unattractive, he may not obstruct her marriage to others in order to inherit her property. In pre-Islamic practice, says Ibn ʿAjība, guardians sought attractive and wealthy orphans without appropriate dowers, while neglecting and obstructing those without beauty or means. Both practices are prohibited.<\/p>\n<p>The helpless children too must be given inheritance shares along with adults, although people had formerly withheld inheritance from minors. He transmits that ʿUyayna ibn Ḥiṣn objected to children and women inheriting when custom had rewarded only those who could fight and gather booty; the Prophet replied that this was what he had been commanded. God also addresses the leaders (<em>al-aʾimma<\/em>), commanding them to administer orphans’ affairs with justice, protect their rights and take precautions for them in all their affairs. God knows the good they perform and rewards them accordingly.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"People ask the Prophet for rulings on women's inheritance and other rights. God commands granting them their rightful shares, already specified earlier in the sūrah, including provisions on the wealth of orphans, marriage of wards and children's inheritance. Guardians must not withhold the dower prescribed for orphan girls and then marry them for less than the dower customarily owing to their peers. If the guardian wishes to marry a beautiful, wealthy orphan, justice requires giving her a suitable dower, otherwise marrying someone else. If he refuses to marry a girl because she is considered unattractive, he may not obstruct her marriage to others in order to inherit her property. In pre-Islamic practice, says Ibn ʿAjība, guardians sought attractive and wealthy orphans without appropriate dowers, while neglecting and obstructing those without beauty or means. Both practices are prohibited. The helpless children too must be given inheritance shares along with adults, although people had formerly withheld inheritance from minors. He transmits that ʿUyayna ibn Ḥiṣn objected to children and women inheriting when custom had rewarded only those who could fight and gather booty; the Prophet replied that this was what he had been commanded. God also addresses the leaders ( al-aʾimma ), commanding them to administer orphans’ affairs with justice, protect their rights and take precautions for them in all their affairs. God knows the good they perform and rewards them accordingly."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>The question can be understood as concerning the “feminine” forms of formal sciences and the “orphan” fruits of the heart, arising from contemplation: divinely granted inward knowledge (<em>ʿilm ladunnī<\/em>) and lordly secrets of the science of reality (<em>ḥaqīqa<\/em>). These are fitting only for those deemed weak among created beings. The saying is cited that the people of Paradise include a weak, despised person whose oath God would fulfil. The response directs the seeker to acquire from formal sciences what is needed to perform worship correctly, to desire the science of the path (<em>ṭarīqa<\/em>) that concerns the heart and establishes servanthood, and to seek from contemplative insights what reveals the Lord's majesty. Justice requires giving the revealed law (<em>sharīʿa<\/em>) its right, the path its right, and guarding the secrets of reality from those unfit to receive them. God does not squander the reward of those who excel.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"The question can be understood as concerning the “feminine” forms of formal sciences and the “orphan” fruits of the heart, arising from contemplation: divinely granted inward knowledge ( ʿilm ladunnī ) and lordly secrets of the science of reality ( ḥaqīqa ). These are fitting only for those deemed weak among created beings. The saying is cited that the people of Paradise include a weak, despised person whose oath God would fulfil. The response directs the seeker to acquire from formal sciences what is needed to perform worship correctly, to desire the science of the path ( ṭarīqa ) that concerns the heart and establishes servanthood, and to seek from contemplative insights what reveals the Lord's majesty. Justice requires giving the revealed law ( sharīʿa ) its right, the path its right, and guarding the secrets of reality from those unfit to receive them. God does not squander the reward of those who excel."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3172,"arabic_characters":3137},{"id":"q4_cont_128","q":"4:128","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:128 · Marital reconciliation and avarice","source":"Cairo I:568","cairo":"I:568–569","source_page_first":568,"source_page_last":569,"ar":"وَإِنِ امْرَأَةٌ خافَتْ مِنْ بَعْلِها نُشُوزاً أَوْ إِعْراضاً فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْهِما أَنْ يُصْلِحا بَيْنَهُما صُلْحاً وَالصُّلْحُ خَيْرٌ وَأُحْضِرَتِ الْأَنْفُسُ الشُّحَّ وَإِنْ تُحْسِنُوا وَتَتَّقُوا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيراً (128)\nقلت: امرأة: فاعل بفعل يُفسره ما بعده، وأصل (يَصَّالِحَا) : يتصالحا، فأدغمت، و (صُلحًا) مصدر. وقرأ الكوفيون: يُصلحا من الرباعي، فتنصب صُلحًا على المفعول به، أو المصدر، و (بينهما) ظرف، أو حال منه، وجملة (الصلح خير) : معترضة، وكذا: وَأُحْضِرَتِ الْأَنْفُسُ الشُّحَّ، ولذلك اغتفر عدم تجانسهما.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَإِنِ امْرَأَةٌ خافَتْ وتوقعت من زوجها نُشُوزاً أي: ترفعًا عن صحبتها، وتجافيًا عنها، كراهية لها، ومنعًا لحقوقها، أَوْ إِعْراضاً عنها، بأن يترك مجالستها، ومحادثتها، فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْهِما أن يتصالحا بَيْنَهُما صُلْحاً بأن تحط له من مهرها، أو من قسمها مع ضرتها، أو تهب له شيئًا تستميله به.\nنزلت في سعد بن الربيع، تزوج على امرأته شابةً، وآثرها عليها. وقيل: في رجل كبرت امرأته، وله معها أولاد، فأراد طلاقها ليتزوج، فقالت له: دعني على أولادي، وأقسم لي في كل شهرين أو أكثر، أو لا تقسم. فذكر ذلك للنبى صلّى الله عليه وسلم فقال له: «قد سمع الله ما تقول، فإن شاء أجابك» ، فنزلت. وقيل: نزلت في سودة زوج النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، لما كبرت، أراد- عليه الصلاة والسلام- أن يُفارقها، فقالت: أمسكني في نسائك ولا تقسم لي، فقد وهبتُ نوبتي لعائشة، فإني أريد أن أُبعث في نسائك.\nثم رغَّب في الصلح فقال: وَالصُّلْحُ خَيْرٌ من المفارقة، أو من سوء العشرة والخصومة، أو خير في نفسه، ولا يكون إلا مع ترك بعض حق النفس من أحد الخصمين، فلذلك ثقل على النفس فشحت به، وإليه أشار بقوله:\nوَأُحْضِرَتِ الْأَنْفُسُ الشُّحَّ أي: جعلته حاضرًا لديها لا يفارقها، لأنها مطبوعة عليه، فالمرأة لا تكاد تسمح للزوج من حقها، ولا تسخو بشيء تعطيه لزوجها، والزوج لا يكاد يصبر على إمساكها وإحسان عشرتها إذا كَرِهها،\nوَإِنْ تُحْسِنُوا العشرة وَتَتَّقُوا النشوز والإعراض ونقص حق المرأة مع كراهة الطبع لها، فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيراً لا يخفى عليه إحسانكم ولا نشوزكم، فيجازي كُلاًّ بعمله، وفي بعض الأثر: «من صبر على أذى زوجته أعطاه الله ثواب أيوب عليه السلام» . وكذلك المرأة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن النفس كالمرأة حين يتزوجها الرجل، فإنها إذا رأت من زوجها الجد في أموره والانقباض عنها، هابته وانقادت لأمره، وإذا رأت منه الليونة والسيولة استخفت بأمره وركبته، وسقطت هيبته من قلبها، فإذا أمرها ونهاها لم تحتفل بأمره، وكذلك النفس إذا رأت من المريد الجد في بدايته والصولة عليها، هابته وانقادت لأمره وكانت له سميعة مطيعة، وإذا رأت منه الرخو والسهولة معها، ركبته وصعب عليه انقيادها وجهادها، فإذا صال عليها وقهرها فأرادت الصلح معه على أن يسامحها في بعض الأمور، وتساعفه فيما يُريد منها، فلا جناح عليهما أن يصالحا بينهما صلحًا، والصلح خير، فإن دوام التشديد قد يفضي إلى الملل، وإن تحسنوا معها بعد معرفتها، وتتقوا الله في سياستها ورياضتها حتى ترد بكم إلى حضرة ربها، فإن الله كان بما تعملون خبيرا.\nثم أمر بالعدل بين النساء، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِنِ امْرَأَةٌ خافَتْ مِنْ بَعْلِها نُشُوزاً أَوْ إِعْراضاً فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْهِما أَنْ يُصْلِحا بَيْنَهُما صُلْحاً وَالصُّلْحُ خَيْرٌ وَأُحْضِرَتِ الْأَنْفُسُ الشُّحَّ وَإِنْ تُحْسِنُوا وَتَتَّقُوا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيراً (128)","quran_en":"If a woman fears aversion or neglect from her husband, there is no blame on the two if they arrange reconciliation between themselves; reconciliation is better. Avarice has been made present to human souls. If you act with excellence and reverent care, God is fully aware of what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>imraʾa<\/em> is the subject of an understood verb explained by the verb following. The original form of “reconcile” (<em>yaṣṣālaḥā<\/em>) is <em>yataṣālaḥā<\/em>, with assimilation; <em>ṣulḥan<\/em> is a verbal noun. The Kūfan reading <em>yuṣliḥā<\/em> is the causative fourth form, in which <em>ṣulḥan<\/em> may be an object or an accusative of action. “Between them” can be adverbial or circumstantial. “Reconciliation is better” and “avarice has been made present to souls” are parenthetic clauses; hence their different formal constructions cause no difficulty.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: imraʾa is the subject of an understood verb explained by the verb following. The original form of “reconcile” ( yaṣṣālaḥā ) is yataṣālaḥā , with assimilation; ṣulḥan is a verbal noun. The Kūfan reading yuṣliḥā is the causative fourth form, in which ṣulḥan may be an object or an accusative of action. “Between them” can be adverbial or circumstantial. “Reconciliation is better” and “avarice has been made present to souls” are parenthetic clauses; hence their different formal constructions cause no difficulty."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>A wife may expect <em>nushūz<\/em>, an aloofness that refuses intimate companionship or neglects her rights because her husband dislikes her, or <em>iʿrāḍ<\/em>, turning away from her in visits or conversation. They may establish an agreed settlement: for example, she may freely waive part of a dower or a share of alternating nights with another wife, or offer something intended to renew their relationship. Ibn ʿAjība reports several occasions of revelation: Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ is said to have favoured a younger woman he married over an existing wife. Another report concerns an older mother who asked not to be divorced, but to remain with her children and receive an occasional night or none. When this was mentioned to the Prophet, he said, “God has heard what you say; if He wills, He will answer you.” The verse was then revealed, according to the report. A further report concerns Sawda: when she grew old, the Prophet wished to separate from her, but she asked to remain among his wives and, according to the account, gave her allotted turn to ʿĀʾisha, saying that she wished to be raised at the Resurrection among his wives.<\/p>\n<p>Reconciliation is better than separation, acrimony or continual hostile cohabitation, and has intrinsic goodness. It can occur only when one of the disputants relinquishes some personal right, which is why the soul finds it burdensome and clings to that right. A wife may not readily surrender claims, and a husband who dislikes her may struggle to maintain kind companionship. God's command nevertheless requires goodness in marital treatment and watchfulness against rejection, neglect and deficient performance of the wife's rights, despite personal aversion. God sees generosity as well as injustice and recompenses accordingly. Ibn ʿAjība also cites an edifying report promising a reward like Job's to one who endures a spouse's hurt. [Translator’s note: the precise attribution of this report has not been verified here.]<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"A wife may expect nushūz , an aloofness that refuses intimate companionship or neglects her rights because her husband dislikes her, or iʿrāḍ , turning away from her in visits or conversation. They may establish an agreed settlement: for example, she may freely waive part of a dower or a share of alternating nights with another wife, or offer something intended to renew their relationship. Ibn ʿAjība reports several occasions of revelation: Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ is said to have favoured a younger woman he married over an existing wife. Another report concerns an older mother who asked not to be divorced, but to remain with her children and receive an occasional night or none. When this was mentioned to the Prophet, he said, “God has heard what you say; if He wills, He will answer you.” The verse was then revealed, according to the report. A further report concerns Sawda: when she grew old, the Prophet wished to separate from her, but she asked to remain among his wives and, according to the account, gave her allotted turn to ʿĀʾisha, saying that she wished to be raised at the Resurrection among his wives. Reconciliation is better than separation, acrimony or continual hostile cohabitation, and has intrinsic goodness. It can occur only when one of the disputants relinquishes some personal right, which is why the soul finds it burdensome and clings to that right. A wife may not readily surrender claims, and a husband who dislikes her may struggle to maintain kind companionship. God's command nevertheless requires goodness in marital treatment and watchfulness against rejection, neglect and deficient performance of the wife's rights, despite personal aversion. God sees generosity as well as injustice and recompenses accordingly. Ibn ʿAjība also cites an edifying report promising a reward like Job's to one who endures a spouse's hurt. [Translator’s note: the precise attribution of this report has not been verified here.]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Know that the lower soul is like a woman when a man marries her. If she sees her husband earnest in his affairs and reserved toward her, she stands in awe of him and obeys his command. If she sees softness and pliancy in him, she makes light of his command and gains the upper hand over him; awe of him falls from her heart, and she pays no heed to his commands and prohibitions. Likewise, when the lower soul sees the novice’s earnestness at the beginning and his forcefulness toward it, it stands in awe of him and listens and obeys. If it sees laxness and leniency in him, it gains the upper hand, and making it obey and struggling against it become difficult. If, after he has assailed and subdued it, it wants to make peace with him on condition that he indulges it in certain matters and it accommodates him in what he wants from it, there is no blame in their making peace, and peace is better: continued severity may lead to weariness. Once you know it, deal well with it and fear God in managing and training it until it brings you into the Presence of its Lord. [Translator’s note: this paragraph applies a marital analogy to training the lower soul; it does not formulate a legal ruling on marital conduct.]<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Know that the lower soul is like a woman when a man marries her. If she sees her husband earnest in his affairs and reserved toward her, she stands in awe of him and obeys his command. If she sees softness and pliancy in him, she makes light of his command and gains the upper hand over him; awe of him falls from her heart, and she pays no heed to his commands and prohibitions. Likewise, when the lower soul sees the novice’s earnestness at the beginning and his forcefulness toward it, it stands in awe of him and listens and obeys. If it sees laxness and leniency in him, it gains the upper hand, and making it obey and struggling against it become difficult. If, after he has assailed and subdued it, it wants to make peace with him on condition that he indulges it in certain matters and it accommodates him in what he wants from it, there is no blame in their making peace, and peace is better: continued severity may lead to weariness. Once you know it, deal well with it and fear God in managing and training it until it brings you into the Presence of its Lord. [Translator’s note: this paragraph applies a marital analogy to training the lower soul; it does not formulate a legal ruling on marital conduct.]"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3683,"arabic_characters":2757},{"id":"q4_cont_129","q":"4:129","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:129 · Equity between wives and human limitation","source":"Cairo I:569","cairo":"I:569–570","source_page_first":569,"source_page_last":570,"ar":"وَلَنْ تَسْتَطِيعُوا أَنْ تَعْدِلُوا بَيْنَ النِّساءِ وَلَوْ حَرَصْتُمْ فَلا تَمِيلُوا كُلَّ الْمَيْلِ فَتَذَرُوها كَالْمُعَلَّقَةِ وَإِنْ تُصْلِحُوا وَتَتَّقُوا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (129)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَنْ تَسْتَطِيعُوا، يا معشر الأزواج، أَنْ تَعْدِلُوا بَيْنَ النِّساءِ العدل الكامل التام في الأقوال والأفعال والنفقة والكسوة والمحبة، وَلَوْ حَرَصْتُمْ على ذلك لضعف حالكم، وقد خففت عنكم، وأسقطت الحرج عنكم، فلا يجب العدل في البيت فقط، وكان صلّى الله عليه وسلم يَقسِمُ بين نِسَائِه فيعِدلُ ويقُولُ: «اللهمَّ هذه قسمتي فِيمَا أملِك، فلا تُؤاخِذني فيمَا لا أملِكُ» ، يعني: ميل القلب، وكان عمر رضي الله عنه يقول: (اللهم قلبي فلا أملكُه، وأما سوى ذلك فإني أرجو أن أعدل) ، وأما الوطء فلا يجب العدل فيه، إلا أن تتحرك شهوته، فيكف لتتوفر لذته للأخرى.\nفَلا تَمِيلُوا إلى المرغوب فيها لجمالها أو شبابها، كُلَّ الْمَيْلِ بالنفقة والكسوة والإقبال عليها، وتَدَعُوا الأخرى كَالْمُعَلَّقَةِ التي ليست ذات بعل ولا مطلقة، كأنها محبوسة مسجونة، وعن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «مَن كانت له أمَرأتانِ يَميلُ معَ إحدَاهما، جَاء يومَ القِيَامَةِ، وأحَدُ شِقّيه مَائِلٌ» ، وَإِنْ تُصْلِحُوا ما كنتم تفسدون في أمورهن بالعدل بينهن، وَتَتَّقُوا الجور فيما يستقبل، فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً، يغفر لكم ما مضى من ميلكم.\nالإشارة: من شأن العبودية: الضعف والعجز، فلا يستطيع العبد أن يقوم بالأمور التي كلف بها على العدل والتمام، ولو حرص كل الحرص، وجدَّ كل الجد، فلا يليق به إلا التحقق بوصفه والرجوع إلى ربه، فيأتي بما يستطيع ولا يحرص على مالا يستطيع، فلا يميل إلى الدعة والكسل كل الميل، ولا يحرص على مالا طاقة له به كل الحرص، فإن التعقيد ليس من شأن أهل التوحيد، بل من شأنهم مساعفة الأقدار، والسكون تحت أحكام الواحد القهار، فلا تميلوا إلى التعمق والتشديد كل الميل، فتتركوا أنفسكم كالمُعلَّقة، أي: المسجونة، وهذا من شأن أهل الحجاب، يُحبسون في المقامات والأحوال، تشغلهم حلاوة ذلك عن الله تعالى. فإذا فقدوا ذلك الحال أو المقام سلبوا وأفلسوا. وأهل الغنى بالله لا يقفون مع حال ولا مقام، هم مع مولاهم، وكل ما يبرز من عنصر القدرة قبلوه، وتلونوا بلونه، وهذا مقام التلوين بعد التمكين.\nوفي إشارة أخرى: اعلم أن القدرة والحكمة كالزوجين للقلب، يقيم عند هذه مدة، وعند هذه أخرى، فإذا أقام عند الحكمة كان في مقام العبودية من جهل وغفلة وضعف وذلة، وإذا أقام عند القدرة كان في مقام شهود الربوبية فيكون في علم ويقظة وقوة وعزة. ولا قدرة له على العدل بينهما، فلا يميل إلى إحداهما كل الميل بل يسير بينهما، ويعطي كل ذي حق حقه، بأن يعرف فضلهما، ويسير بكل واحد منهما. وإن تصلحوا قلوبكم وتتقوا ما يشغلكم عن ربكم، فإن الله كان غفورًا رحيمًا يغفر لكم ميلكم إلى إحدى الجهتين والله تعالى أعلم.\nفإذا تعذر الإصلاح بين الزوجين، وأراد الفراق ففى الله الغنى عن كل شيء، كما أشار إلى ذلك بقوله:","quran_ar":"وَلَنْ تَسْتَطِيعُوا أَنْ تَعْدِلُوا بَيْنَ النِّساءِ وَلَوْ حَرَصْتُمْ فَلا تَمِيلُوا كُلَّ الْمَيْلِ فَتَذَرُوها كَالْمُعَلَّقَةِ وَإِنْ تُصْلِحُوا وَتَتَّقُوا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (129)","quran_en":"You shall never be able to deal with wives in perfect equality, however earnestly you seek it. But do not incline entirely to one and leave another suspended, neither truly married nor free. If you restore matters and remain mindful of God, God is Forgiving, Merciful.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Husbands cannot achieve absolute equality in speech, actions, spending, clothing and inward affection, no matter how they strive, because of the weakness of the human condition. He then says, in the divine first person, “I have lightened [the requirement] for you and removed hardship from you,” followed in the printed text by, “Equality in the house alone is not obligatory.” [Textual note: Cairo I:569 reads <span lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فلا يجب العدل في البيت فقط<\/span>. Besides “house,” <em>bayt<\/em> can mean spending the night: <em>Lisān al-ʿArab<\/em>, II:16, lists it among the verbal nouns of <em>bāta<\/em>. The following account of dividing turns among wives makes that sense plausible here. It does not settle the scope of “not” and “only,” however. Earlier parallels in Ibn Juzayy and al-Bayḍāwī explain the concession for unattainable perfect equality but do not supply this disputed clause.] Ibn ʿAjība cites the reported supplication of the Prophet when distributing his turns among his wives: “O God, this is my division concerning what I control; do not hold me to account for what I do not control,” meaning the heart's leaning. ʿUmar is quoted similarly: the heart is not his to command, but in other affairs he hopes to act justly. Ibn ʿAjība says absolute equality in intercourse is not imposed, though a husband must not deliberately reserve all his sensual energy for one wife to the neglect of another.<\/p>\n<p>Do not incline so completely toward a favoured younger or more attractive wife in clothing, maintenance and attention that the other is left “suspended”—not enjoying actual married companionship, yet not divorced and free to form a new household. The Prophet is reported to warn that the man who shows unjust partiality toward one of two wives will appear on the Day of Resurrection with one side inclined. If husbands correct previous unfairness and abstain from injustice in future, God is merciful and forgives what has passed.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Husbands cannot achieve absolute equality in speech, actions, spending, clothing and inward affection, no matter how they strive, because of the weakness of the human condition. He then says, in the divine first person, “I have lightened [the requirement] for you and removed hardship from you,” followed in the printed text by, “Equality in the house alone is not obligatory.” [Textual note: Cairo I:569 reads فلا يجب العدل في البيت فقط . Besides “house,” bayt can mean spending the night: Lisān al-ʿArab , II:16, lists it among the verbal nouns of bāta . The following account of dividing turns among wives makes that sense plausible here. It does not settle the scope of “not” and “only,” however. Earlier parallels in Ibn Juzayy and al-Bayḍāwī explain the concession for unattainable perfect equality but do not supply this disputed clause.] Ibn ʿAjība cites the reported supplication of the Prophet when distributing his turns among his wives: “O God, this is my division concerning what I control; do not hold me to account for what I do not control,” meaning the heart's leaning. ʿUmar is quoted similarly: the heart is not his to command, but in other affairs he hopes to act justly. Ibn ʿAjība says absolute equality in intercourse is not imposed, though a husband must not deliberately reserve all his sensual energy for one wife to the neglect of another. Do not incline so completely toward a favoured younger or more attractive wife in clothing, maintenance and attention that the other is left “suspended”—not enjoying actual married companionship, yet not divorced and free to form a new household. The Prophet is reported to warn that the man who shows unjust partiality toward one of two wives will appear on the Day of Resurrection with one side inclined. If husbands correct previous unfairness and abstain from injustice in future, God is merciful and forgives what has passed."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"capacity and moderation","html":"<p>Weakness and incapacity are among the essential marks of servanthood. However diligent a person becomes, complete perfection in all responsibilities is beyond him. He should recognise his proper condition, return to the Lord and do what is within capacity, without surrendering entirely to laziness or trying to accomplish what he has no power to bear. Excessive self-imposed complication is not the way of the people of divine unity; they consent to decrees and rest beneath the judgment of the irresistible One. Do not incline wholly to spiritual severity, leaving the soul suspended and imprisoned. The veiled can become detained within spiritual stations and states, distracted from God by their sweetness; losing those states then leaves them bereft. Those enriched through God do not stop with any state or station: whatever the divine power unfolds, they receive it and assume its appropriate colour. This is <em>talwīn<\/em>—responsive change—after <em>tamkīn<\/em>, spiritual establishment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Another spiritual allusion.<\/strong> Divine power (<em>qudra<\/em>) and wisdom (<em>ḥikma<\/em>) are like two spouses to the heart, which dwells for a time with one and then with the other. With wisdom it is in the station of servanthood, marked by ignorance, heedlessness, weakness and lowliness; with power it witnesses lordship, characterised by knowledge, awakening, strength and dignity. The human being cannot deal equally with the two, yet should not abandon one wholly for the other. Both must be recognised and given their appropriate rights. If you purify your hearts and remain attentive to what distracts from the Lord, He forgives your leaning toward one of the two sides. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Weakness and incapacity are among the essential marks of servanthood. However diligent a person becomes, complete perfection in all responsibilities is beyond him. He should recognise his proper condition, return to the Lord and do what is within capacity, without surrendering entirely to laziness or trying to accomplish what he has no power to bear. Excessive self-imposed complication is not the way of the people of divine unity; they consent to decrees and rest beneath the judgment of the irresistible One. Do not incline wholly to spiritual severity, leaving the soul suspended and imprisoned. The veiled can become detained within spiritual stations and states, distracted from God by their sweetness; losing those states then leaves them bereft. Those enriched through God do not stop with any state or station: whatever the divine power unfolds, they receive it and assume its appropriate colour. This is talwīn —responsive change—after tamkīn , spiritual establishment. Another spiritual allusion. Divine power ( qudra ) and wisdom ( ḥikma ) are like two spouses to the heart, which dwells for a time with one and then with the other. With wisdom it is in the station of servanthood, marked by ignorance, heedlessness, weakness and lowliness; with power it witnesses lordship, characterised by knowledge, awakening, strength and dignity. The human being cannot deal equally with the two, yet should not abandon one wholly for the other. Both must be recognised and given their appropriate rights. If you purify your hearts and remain attentive to what distracts from the Lord, He forgives your leaning toward one of the two sides. God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3555,"arabic_characters":2561},{"id":"q4_cont_130","q":"4:130","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:130 · Separation and God’s sufficiency","source":"Cairo I:570–571","cairo":"I:570–571","source_page_first":570,"source_page_last":571,"ar":"وَإِنْ يَتَفَرَّقا يُغْنِ اللَّهُ كُلاًّ مِنْ سَعَتِهِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ واسِعاً حَكِيماً (130) وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ ...\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَإِنْ يَتَفَرَّقا أي: يفارق كل واحد منهما صاحبه، يُغْنِ اللَّهُ كل واحد منهما عن صاحبه، ببدل أو سُلُو يقوم بأمره من رزق أو غيره، من سعة غناه وكمال قدرته، وَكانَ اللَّهُ واسِعاً بقدرته حَكِيماً أي: متقنًا في أحكامه وأفعاله. ثم بيَّن معنى سعته فقال: وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ أي: كل ما استقر فيهما فهو تحت حكمه ومشيئته، قائمًا بحفظه وتدبيره، يعطي كل واحد ما يقوم بأمره ويغنيه عن غيره. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن الروح ما دامت مسجونة تحت قهر البشرية، محجوبةً عن شهود معاني الربوبية، كانت فقيرة جائعة متعطشة، تتعشق إلى الأكوان وتفتقر إليها، وتقف معها، فإذا فارقت البشرية وانطلقت من سجن هيكلها،\nوخرجت فكرتها من سجن الأكوان، أغناها الله بشهود ذاته، وأفضت إلى سعة فضاء الشهود والعيان، وملكت جميع الأكوان، «أنت مع الأكوان ما لم تشهد المكون، فإذا شهدت المكون كانت الأكوان معك» ، وكذلك البشرية يغنيها الله عن تعب الخدمة وتستريح في ظل المعرفة، فلما تفرقا أغنى الله كلاًّ من سعة فضله وجوده، لأنه واسع العطاء والجود، حكيم في تدبير إمداد كل موجود.\n\nوفي قوله: وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ إشارة إلى أنَّ مَن كان بالله، ووصل إلى شهود ذاته، ملَّكه الله ما في السموات وما في الأرض، فيكون خليفة الله في ملكه، (وما ذلك على الله بعزيز) .\nولمّا جرى الكلام على شأن النساء، وهن حبائل الشيطان، تشغل فتنتهن عن ذكر الرحمن، حذَّر الحق تعالى من فتنتهن، كما هو عادته تعالى في كتابه عند ذكرهن، وأمر بالتقوى التي هي حصن من كل فتنة، فقال:\n... وَلَقَدْ وَصَّيْنَا الَّذِينَ أُوتُوا الْكِتابَ مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ وَإِيَّاكُمْ أَنِ اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَإِنْ تَكْفُرُوا فَإِنَّ لِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ غَنِيًّا حَمِيداً (131)","quran_ar":"وَإِنْ يَتَفَرَّقا يُغْنِ اللَّهُ كُلاًّ مِنْ سَعَتِهِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ واسِعاً حَكِيماً (130)","quran_en":"If the two separate, God will enrich each out of His abundance. God is Vast and Wise.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>If husband and wife separate, God will make each independent of the other by another suitable partner, consolation or sustaining provision from His boundless generosity. He is vast in power and precise in every ordinance and deed. The following declaration that everything in the heavens and earth belongs to Him explains the source of that abundance: every creature lives under His will, protection and governance. He gives each whatever is necessary to sustain them and frees them from dependence upon another. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"If husband and wife separate, God will make each independent of the other by another suitable partner, consolation or sustaining provision from His boundless generosity. He is vast in power and precise in every ordinance and deed. The following declaration that everything in the heavens and earth belongs to Him explains the source of that abundance: every creature lives under His will, protection and governance. He gives each whatever is necessary to sustain them and frees them from dependence upon another. God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>So long as the spirit remains imprisoned beneath the domination of bodily human nature and veiled from the witnessing of lordship, it remains hungry, thirsty and needy, attaching itself passionately to created objects. When it separates from that tyranny and departs from the prison of bodily form, its reflective faculty leaves the imprisonment of created appearances. God makes it wealthy through the witnessing of His Essence, granting it the ample space of direct vision and presence. It comes to possess all created things. It is said: “You are with created things as long as you do not witness their Creator; once you witness the Creator, created things are with you.” God also frees bodily nature from the fatigue of service, and it rests in the shade of knowledge. Through that separation each receives sufficiency out of God's bounty, for He is boundless in generosity and wise in arranging provision for every existent.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"So long as the spirit remains imprisoned beneath the domination of bodily human nature and veiled from the witnessing of lordship, it remains hungry, thirsty and needy, attaching itself passionately to created objects. When it separates from that tyranny and departs from the prison of bodily form, its reflective faculty leaves the imprisonment of created appearances. God makes it wealthy through the witnessing of His Essence, granting it the ample space of direct vision and presence. It comes to possess all created things. It is said: “You are with created things as long as you do not witness their Creator; once you witness the Creator, created things are with you.” God also frees bodily nature from the fatigue of service, and it rests in the shade of knowledge. Through that separation each receives sufficiency out of God's bounty, for He is boundless in generosity and wise in arranging provision for every existent."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1457,"arabic_characters":1735},{"id":"q4_cont_131","q":"4:131–133","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:131–133 · The imperative of reverent awareness","source":"Cairo I:570–573","cairo":"I:571–573","source_page_first":571,"source_page_last":573,"ar":"وَلِلَّهِ مَا فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ وَكِيلاً (132) إِنْ يَشَأْ يُذْهِبْكُمْ أَيُّهَا النَّاسُ وَيَأْتِ بِآخَرِينَ وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلى ذلِكَ قَدِيراً (133)\nقلت: (من قبلكم) : يتعلق بأوتوا أو بوصينا، و (إياكم) : عطف على الذين، و (أن اتقوا) : على حذف الجار، أي:\nبأن اتقوا، أو مفسرة لأن التوصية في معنى القول، و (إن تكفروا) على حذف القول، أي: وقلنا لهم ولكم: وإن تكفروا ... الخ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَقَدْ وَصَّيْنَا الأمم المتقدمة الذين أنزلنا عليهم الْكِتابَ مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ كأهل التوراة والإنجيل والزبور، وغيرهم من الأمم، ووصيناكم أنتم أَنِ اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ بأن تمتثلوا أوامره، وتجتنبوا نواهيَه، ظاهرًا وباطنًا، وقلنا لهم ولكم: وَإِنْ تَكْفُرُوا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ عَنِ كفركم وشكركم فقد استقر له ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ مُلكاً وعبيدًا، فله فيهما من الملائكة من هو أطوع منكم، فلا يتضرر بكفركم، كما لا ينتفع بشكركم وتقواكم، وإنما أوصاكم رحمًة بكم، لا لحاجة إليكم، ثم قرر ذلك بقوله: وَكانَ اللَّهُ غَنِيًّا حَمِيداً أي: غنيًا عن الخلق وعبادتهم، محمودًا في ذاته، حُمِد أو لم يُحمد.\nوَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ كرره ثالثًا للدلالة على كونه غنيًا حميدًا، فإن جميع المخلوقات تدل بحاجتها على غناه، وبما أفاض عليها من الوجود، وأنواع الخصائص والكمالات على كونه حميدًا.\nقاله البيضاوي. وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ وَكِيلًا أي: حافظًا ومجيرًا لمن تعلق به من أهل السموات والأرض. إِنْ يَشَأْ يُذْهِبْكُمْ أَيُّهَا النَّاسُ إن لم تتقوه، ويأت بقوم آخرين، هم أطوع منكم وأتقى، وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلى ذلِكَ قَدِيراً أي: بليغ القدرة لا يعجزه مُراد.\nقال البيضاوي: وهذا- أي قوله: (إن يشأ يذهبكم..) - أيضًا تقرير لغناه وقدرته، وتهديد لمن كفر وخالف أمره، وقيل: هو خطاب لمن خالف الرسول صلّى الله عليه وسلم من العرب، وهو معنى قوله: وَإِنْ تَتَوَلَّوْا يَسْتَبْدِلْ قَوْماً غَيْرَكُمْ لما رُوي: أنها لمّا نزلت ضرب رسول صلّى الله عليه وسلم يدَه على ظهر سَلمَان وقال: «إنّهم قَومُ هذا» .\nالإشارة: التقوى أساس الطريق ومنهاج أهل التحقيق، عليها سلك السائرون، وبها وصل الواصلون، قد وصَّى بها الحق تعالى المتقدمين والمتأخرين، وبها قرّب المقربين وشرّف المكرمين. ولها خَمسُ درجاتٍ: أن يتقي العبد الكفر وذلك بمقام الإسلام، وأن يتقي المعاصي والمحرمات وهو: مقام التوبة، وأن يتقي الشبهات وهو مقام الورع، وأن يتقي المباحات، وهو مقام الزهد، وأن يتقي شهود السَّوى والحس وهو مقام المشاهدة.\nولها فضائل مستنبطة من القرآن، وهي خمس عشرة: الهداية لقوله تعالى: هُدىً لِلْمُتَّقِينَ، والنصرة لقوله: إِنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الَّذِينَ اتَّقَوْا، والولاية لقوله: وَاللَّهُ وَلِيُّ الْمُتَّقِينَ والمحبة لقوله: إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَّقِينَ، وتنوير القلب لقوله: يَآ أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِنْ تَتَّقُوا اللَّهَ يَجْعَلْ لَكُمْ فُرْقاناً، والمخرج من الغم والرزق من حيث لا يحتسب، لقوله: وَمَنْ يَتَّقِ اللَّهَ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ مَخْرَجاً، وَيَرْزُقْهُ مِنْ حَيْثُ لا يَحْتَسِبُ، وتيسير الأمور لقوله: وَمَنْ يَتَّقِ اللَّهَ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ مِنْ أَمْرِهِ يُسْراً وغفران الذنوب وإعظام الأجر لقوله: وَمَنْ يَتَّقِ اللَّهَ يُكَفِّرْ عَنْهُ سَيِّئاتِهِ وَيُعْظِمْ لَهُ أَجْراً، وتقبل الأعمال لقوله: إِنَّما يَتَقَبَّلُ اللَّهُ مِنَ الْمُتَّقِينَ والفلاح لقوله: وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ والبشرى لقوله: لَهُمُ الْبُشْرى فِي الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا وَفِي الْآخِرَةِ\n، ودخول الجنة لقوله: إِنَّ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ جَنَّاتِ النَّعِيمِ، والنجاة من النار لقوله: ثُمَّ نُنَجِّي الَّذِينَ اتَّقَوْا. هـ. من ابن جزي.\nومما ينسب للقطب ابن مشيش رضي الله عنه:\nعليكَ بتقوى الله في السرِّ والجهرِ ... ذا شئتَ توفيقَا إلى سُبُلِ الخيرِ\nلأن التُّقى أصلٌ إلى البِرَّ كلَّه ... فخُذه تَفُز بكلِّ نوعٍ من البرّ\nوخيرُ جميعِ الزاد ما قال ربُّنا فَكُن ... يا أخي للهِ مُمتَثِل الأمر\nولمّا قرر أن الملك كله بيده، رغّب الناس فى رفع حوائجهم إليه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَلَقَدْ وَصَّيْنَا الَّذِينَ أُوتُوا الْكِتابَ مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ وَإِيَّاكُمْ أَنِ اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَإِنْ تَكْفُرُوا فَإِنَّ لِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ غَنِيًّا حَمِيداً (131) وَلِلَّهِ مَا فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ وَكِيلاً (132) إِنْ يَشَأْ يُذْهِبْكُمْ أَيُّهَا النَّاسُ وَيَأْتِ بِآخَرِينَ وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلى ذلِكَ قَدِيراً (133)","quran_en":"Everything in the heavens and earth belongs to God. We instructed those given the Book before you, and you yourselves, to remain mindful of God. If you disbelieve, whatever is in the heavens and earth belongs to God, who is Self-Sufficient and Praiseworthy. Everything in the heavens and earth belongs to God, and God is enough as Guardian. If He wills, He can remove you, O humanity, and bring others in your place. God is fully able to do so.","segments":[{"type":"spiritual","title":"Abiding through God","html":"<p>Ibn ʿAjība finds an allusion in “To God belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth”: whoever abides through God and attains the witnessing of His Essence, God gives him dominion over whatever is in the heavens and the earth, and he becomes God’s deputy in His kingdom. “That is not difficult for God.”<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Ibn ʿAjība finds an allusion in “To God belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth”: whoever abides through God and attains the witnessing of His Essence, God gives him dominion over whatever is in the heavens and the earth, and he becomes God’s deputy in His kingdom. “That is not difficult for God.”"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>After speaking of women, whom he calls “Satan’s snares,” Ibn ʿAjība says that their temptation distracts from remembrance of the All-Merciful. He says that God therefore warns against their temptation, as is His custom in His Book whenever He mentions them. [Translator’s note: this description of women belongs to the historical source’s ascetic rhetoric.] God then commands <em>taqwā<\/em>, the protection against every trial.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"After speaking of women, whom he calls “Satan’s snares,” Ibn ʿAjība says that their temptation distracts from remembrance of the All-Merciful. He says that God therefore warns against their temptation, as is His custom in His Book whenever He mentions them. [Translator’s note: this description of women belongs to the historical source’s ascetic rhetoric.] God then commands taqwā , the protection against every trial."},{"type":"language","title":"The construction of the instruction","html":"<p>I say: “before you” may attach either to “were given” or to “We instructed”; “and you” is coordinated with “those.” “To fear God” may have an omitted governing preposition, or <em>an<\/em> may introduce what the instruction consists in. “If you disbelieve” follows an understood “We said to them and to you.”<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “before you” may attach either to “were given” or to “We instructed”; “and you” is coordinated with “those.” “To fear God” may have an omitted governing preposition, or an may introduce what the instruction consists in. “If you disbelieve” follows an understood “We said to them and to you.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>All preceding communities entrusted with revelation—the communities of Torah, Gospel and Psalms, and others—were instructed, as Muslims are now, to obey God's commands and avoid His prohibitions inwardly and outwardly. They are told that He has no need for either their unbelief or thanksgiving. All heavens and earth and obedient angels already belong to Him. Their denial does not diminish Him and their gratitude does not increase Him: the instruction proceeds from mercy for their welfare, not from divine need. “Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy” means entirely independent of creation and intrinsically deserving praise, whether anyone praises Him or not.<\/p>\n<p>The repetition of the ownership of heavens and earth for a third time, al-Bayḍāwī explains, strengthens both attributes: the neediness of creatures demonstrates His sufficiency, while the existence and particular perfections He bestows disclose why He is praised. As <em>Wakīl<\/em>, He protects those who entrust themselves to Him. If human beings resist the demand for <em>taqwā<\/em>, He can replace them with another people more devoted and obedient. Al-Bayḍāwī sees a further proof of God's sufficiency and power and a warning to those who deny His command. One interpretation addresses Arabs opposing the Prophet, alongside “If you turn away, He will replace you with another people” (47:38). Ibn ʿAjība cites the report that the Prophet placed his hand on Salmān's back and said, “These are that man's people.”<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"All preceding communities entrusted with revelation—the communities of Torah, Gospel and Psalms, and others—were instructed, as Muslims are now, to obey God's commands and avoid His prohibitions inwardly and outwardly. They are told that He has no need for either their unbelief or thanksgiving. All heavens and earth and obedient angels already belong to Him. Their denial does not diminish Him and their gratitude does not increase Him: the instruction proceeds from mercy for their welfare, not from divine need. “Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy” means entirely independent of creation and intrinsically deserving praise, whether anyone praises Him or not. The repetition of the ownership of heavens and earth for a third time, al-Bayḍāwī explains, strengthens both attributes: the neediness of creatures demonstrates His sufficiency, while the existence and particular perfections He bestows disclose why He is praised. As Wakīl , He protects those who entrust themselves to Him. If human beings resist the demand for taqwā , He can replace them with another people more devoted and obedient. Al-Bayḍāwī sees a further proof of God's sufficiency and power and a warning to those who deny His command. One interpretation addresses Arabs opposing the Prophet, alongside “If you turn away, He will replace you with another people” (47:38). Ibn ʿAjība cites the report that the Prophet placed his hand on Salmān's back and said, “These are that man's people.”"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"the degrees of *taqwā*","html":"<p>God-consciousness is the foundation of the path and the method of those who realise the truth. Through it wayfarers travel and people of arrival reach their goal; God enjoined it upon earlier and later communities and by it brought His chosen servants near. It has five degrees: guarding against unbelief, the station of Islam; guarding against prohibited deeds, the station of repentance; guarding against doubtful matters, the station of scrupulousness (<em>waraʿ<\/em>); guarding against permissible things, the station of renunciation (<em>zuhd<\/em>); and guarding against witnessing what is other than God and sensory appearances, the station of witnessing (<em>mushāhada<\/em>).<\/p>\n<p>He enumerates fifteen virtues associated with <em>taqwā<\/em> from Qurʾānic verses (following Ibn Juzayy): guidance (2:2); divine help and victory (16:128); God's protective friendship (45:19); divine love (3:76); illumination and discernment (<em>furqān<\/em>, 8:29); an exit from distress and provision from unexpected sources (65:2–3); ease in difficult affairs (65:4); removal of sins and augmentation of reward (65:5); acceptance of good works (5:27); success (5:100); glad tidings in this life and the next (10:64); gardens of bliss (68:34); and salvation from the Fire (19:72). [Translator’s note: the count of fifteen separates the paired effects—relief from distress and unexpected provision, and forgiveness of sins and enlargement of reward—rather than counting the cited verses.]<\/p>\n<p>A poem attributed to Ibn Mashīsh is then cited:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Hold fast to God-consciousness in secret and in public,<br>if you desire success upon the pathways of the good;<br>reverent care is the root of every act of righteousness,<br>so grasp it and obtain each kind of excellence.<br>The best provision is that which our Lord declared:<br>my brother, therefore, obey God's command.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Since all sovereignty belongs to Him, the next verse encourages people to take their needs directly to Him.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"God-consciousness is the foundation of the path and the method of those who realise the truth. Through it wayfarers travel and people of arrival reach their goal; God enjoined it upon earlier and later communities and by it brought His chosen servants near. It has five degrees: guarding against unbelief, the station of Islam; guarding against prohibited deeds, the station of repentance; guarding against doubtful matters, the station of scrupulousness ( waraʿ ); guarding against permissible things, the station of renunciation ( zuhd ); and guarding against witnessing what is other than God and sensory appearances, the station of witnessing ( mushāhada ). He enumerates fifteen virtues associated with taqwā from Qurʾānic verses (following Ibn Juzayy): guidance (2:2); divine help and victory (16:128); God's protective friendship (45:19); divine love (3:76); illumination and discernment ( furqān , 8:29); an exit from distress and provision from unexpected sources (65:2–3); ease in difficult affairs (65:4); removal of sins and augmentation of reward (65:5); acceptance of good works (5:27); success (5:100); glad tidings in this life and the next (10:64); gardens of bliss (68:34); and salvation from the Fire (19:72). [Translator’s note: the count of fifteen separates the paired effects—relief from distress and unexpected provision, and forgiveness of sins and enlargement of reward—rather than counting the cited verses.] A poem attributed to Ibn Mashīsh is then cited: Hold fast to God-consciousness in secret and in public, if you desire success upon the pathways of the good; reverent care is the root of every act of righteousness, so grasp it and obtain each kind of excellence. The best provision is that which our Lord declared: my brother, therefore, obey God's command. Since all sovereignty belongs to Him, the next verse encourages people to take their needs directly to Him."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4381,"arabic_characters":3543},{"id":"q4_cont_134","q":"4:134","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:134 · Reward in this world and the next","source":"Cairo I:573","cairo":"I:573","source_page_first":573,"source_page_last":573,"ar":"مَنْ كانَ يُرِيدُ ثَوابَ الدُّنْيا فَعِنْدَ اللَّهِ ثَوابُ الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ سَمِيعاً بَصِيراً (134)\nقلت: (من) : شرطية، وجوابها محذوف دل عليه الكلام، أي: مَن كان يريد ثواب الدنيا فليطلبه منه، فعند الله ثواب الدنيا والآخرة، أو من كان يريد ثواب الدنيا فلا يقتصر عليه خاصة، فعند الله ثواب الدنيا والآخرة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: مَنْ كانَ يُرِيدُ ثَوابَ الدُّنْيا والتوسع فيها، فليطلبه منا فعند الله ثواب الدارين، أو من كان يريد ثواب الدنيا، فليطلب مع ذلك ثوابَ الآخرة أيضًا، وليقل: رَبَّنا آتِنا فِي الدُّنْيا حَسَنَةً وَفِي الْآخِرَةِ حَسَنَةً فَعِنْدَ اللَّهِ ثَوابُ الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ، فيعطيهما معًا لمن طلبهما، والثاني أنهض من الأول، وأكملُ منهما من أعرض عنهما وطلب مولاه، وَكانَ اللَّهُ سَمِيعاً بَصِيراً، لا يخفى عليه مقاصد خلقه، فيعطي كُلاًّ على حسب قصده.\nالإشارة: الهمم ثلاثة: همة دنية تعلقت بالدنيا الدنية، وهمة متوسطة تعلقت بنعيم الآخرة، وهمة عالية تعلقت بالكبير المتعال. والله تعالى يرزق العبد على قدر همته، وبالهمم ترفع المقادير أو تسقط، فمن كانت همته دنية كان دَنيًا خسيسًا، ومن كانت همته متوسطة كان قدره متوسطًا، رحل من كون إلى كون، كحمار الرحا، يسير، والذي ارتحل منه هو الذي عاد إليه، ومن كانت همته عالية كان عالي المقدار، كبير الشأن، حاز الكونين بما فيهما، وزاد مشاهدة خالقهما. جعلنا الله منهم بمنِّه وكرمه.\nولما أمر بالعدل بين النساء أمر بالعدل فى الأحكام كلها، فقال:","quran_ar":"مَنْ كانَ يُرِيدُ ثَوابَ الدُّنْيا فَعِنْدَ اللَّهِ ثَوابُ الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ سَمِيعاً بَصِيراً (134)","quran_en":"Whoever desires the reward of this world—with God rests the reward of this world and of the next. God is Hearing, Seeing.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>man<\/em> is conditional, with its answer omitted but made clear by what follows: whoever desires worldly reward should ask for it from God, since He possesses both worlds; or one seeking worldly recompense should not confine the aspiration to that alone, since the reward of both worlds is with God. A person who desires worldly ease should seek it from God, or better should also seek the hereafter, praying “Our Lord, grant us good in the world and good in the hereafter” (2:201). God gives both together to whoever seeks them. The second understanding is more uplifting than the first. More complete than either is the person who turns away from both and seeks the Lord Himself. God hears and sees the intentions of creatures and bestows what accords with their purposes.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: man is conditional, with its answer omitted but made clear by what follows: whoever desires worldly reward should ask for it from God, since He possesses both worlds; or one seeking worldly recompense should not confine the aspiration to that alone, since the reward of both worlds is with God. A person who desires worldly ease should seek it from God, or better should also seek the hereafter, praying “Our Lord, grant us good in the world and good in the hereafter” (2:201). God gives both together to whoever seeks them. The second understanding is more uplifting than the first. More complete than either is the person who turns away from both and seeks the Lord Himself. God hears and sees the intentions of creatures and bestows what accords with their purposes."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Aspirations (<em>himam<\/em>) are three: lowly aspiration attached to the lower world; middle aspiration seeking the delights of the hereafter; and high aspiration directed to the Most High. God provides to a servant in proportion to the scope of aspiration, and through aspiration stations rise or fall. Whoever desires the lowest is correspondingly low in rank. The middle aspirant passes from one created condition to another, like a mill-donkey turning around its wheel and arriving again at the same place. A person of lofty aspiration rises high, attains what both worlds contain, and in addition receives vision of their Creator. May God make us among them through bounty and generosity. The commentary next extends the demand for justice from marriage to all judgments.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Aspirations ( himam ) are three: lowly aspiration attached to the lower world; middle aspiration seeking the delights of the hereafter; and high aspiration directed to the Most High. God provides to a servant in proportion to the scope of aspiration, and through aspiration stations rise or fall. Whoever desires the lowest is correspondingly low in rank. The middle aspirant passes from one created condition to another, like a mill-donkey turning around its wheel and arriving again at the same place. A person of lofty aspiration rises high, attains what both worlds contain, and in addition receives vision of their Creator. May God make us among them through bounty and generosity. The commentary next extends the demand for justice from marriage to all judgments."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1545,"arabic_characters":1303},{"id":"q4_cont_135","q":"4:135","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:135 · Testimony, impartiality, and justice","source":"Cairo I:573–575","cairo":"I:573–575","source_page_first":573,"source_page_last":575,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا كُونُوا قَوَّامِينَ بِالْقِسْطِ شُهَداءَ لِلَّهِ وَلَوْ عَلى أَنْفُسِكُمْ أَوِ الْوالِدَيْنِ وَالْأَقْرَبِينَ إِنْ يَكُنْ غَنِيًّا أَوْ فَقِيراً فَاللَّهُ أَوْلى بِهِما فَلا تَتَّبِعُوا الْهَوى أَنْ تَعْدِلُوا وَإِنْ تَلْوُوا أَوْ تُعْرِضُوا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيراً (135)\nقلت: (شهداء) : خبر ثان لكان، أو حال، (فالله أولى) : علة للجواب أي: إن يكن المشهود عليه غنيًا عليه فلا تمتنعوا من الشهادة عليه تعظيمًا له، وإن يكن فقيرًا فلا تمتنعوا من الشهادة عليه إشفاقًا عليه، فإن الله أولى بالغني والفقير منكم، والضمير في (بهما) راجع إلى مادل عليه المذكور، وهو جنسا الغني والفقير، لا إليه وإلا لوحّد لأن «أو» لأحد الشيئين. و (أن تعدلوا) : مفعول من أجله، ومن قرأ: تلوا- بضم اللام- فقد نقل ضم الواو إلى اللام وحذف أحد الواوين، وقيل: من الولاية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا كُونُوا قَوَّامِينَ بِالْقِسْطِ أي: مجتهدين في إقامة العدل مواظبين على الحكم به، وكونوا شُهَداءَ لِلَّهِ بالحق تقيمون شهادتكم لوجه الله، وابتغاء مرضاته، بلا طمع أجر ولا عوض، وهذا إن تعينت عليه، ولم يكن في تحملها مشقة، وإلا أُبيح له أجر تعبه، فأدوا شهاداتكم وَلَوْ كانت عَلى أَنْفُسِكُمْ بأن تقروا بالحق الذي عليها، لأن الشهادة بيان الحق، سواء كان عليها أو على غيرها، أَوِ كانت الشهادة على الْوالِدَيْنِ وَالْأَقْرَبِينَ، فلا تمنعكم الشفقة والتعظيم من إقامة الشهادة عليهما، وأحرى غيرهما من الأجانب، إِنْ يَكُنْ المشهود عليه غَنِيًّا أَوْ فَقِيراً فلا تميلوا عن الشهادة بالحق عليهما، تعظيمًا للغني أو شفقة للفقير، فإن الله أَوْلى بِهِما وبالنظر لهما، فلو لم تكن الشهادة عليهما صلاحًا لهما ما شرعها، فَلا تَتَّبِعُوا الْهَوى فتميلوا مع الغني أو الفقير، فقد نهيتكم إرادة أَنْ تَعْدِلُوا في أحكامكم، فتكونوا عدولاً، أو كراهية أن تعدلوا عن الحق أي: تميلوا، وَإِنْ تَلْوُوا ألسنتكم عن شهادة الحق أو حكومة العدل أَوْ تُعْرِضُوا عن أدائها فتكتموها فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيراً، فيجازي الكاتم والمؤدي.\nقال صلّى الله عليه وسلم عند نزولها: «مَن كانَ يؤمن بالله واليوم الآخر فليُقِم شهادتَه على من كانت، ومن كان يؤمن بالله واليوم الآخر، فلا يَجحَد حَقًا هو عليه، وليؤُده عَفوّا، ولا يلجئه إلى السُلطان وخصوُمتِه، ليقتطع بها حقه، وأيما رجل خَاصَمَ إليَّ فقضَيتُ له على أخِيِه بحقٍ ليس له عليه، فلا يأخُذُه، فإنَما أقَطَعُ له قطعةً مِنَ النَارِ» .\nالإشارة: قد أمر الحق تعالى عباده بإقامة العدل في الأمور كلها، ونهى عن مراقبة الخلق في الأشياء كلها، فيتأكد على المريد ألاَّ يراقب أحدًا من الخلق وإنما يراقب الملك الحق، فيكون قويًا في الحق، يقيمه على نفسه وغيره، فلا تجتمع مراقبة الحق مع مراقبة الخلق، من راقب الحق غاب عن الناس، ومن راقب الناس غاب عن الحق، وعاش مغمومًا من الخلق، ولله در القائل حيث قال:\nمن رَاقَبَ الناسَ مات غما ... وفاز باللذات الجَسُور\nوكان شيخ شيخنا رضي الله عنه يقول: (مراقبةُ الخلقِ عند أهل الظاهر شيءٌ كبير، وعدم المراقبة عند الباطن أمر كبير) . فإقامة العدل على النفس ألاَّ يتركها تميل إلى الرخص والتأويلات، وإقامته على الوالدين تذكيرهما بالله ودلالتهما على الله بلطف ولين، وإقامته على الأقربين بنصحهم وإرشادهم إلى ما فيه صلاحهم، كانوا أغنياء أو فقراء، وإقامته على الأجانب كذلك. وبالله التوفيق.\nولما فرغ مما يتعلق بحفظ اللسان، تكلم على حفظ الإيمان، وهو الأمر السادس مما تضمنته السورة، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا كُونُوا قَوَّامِينَ بِالْقِسْطِ شُهَداءَ لِلَّهِ وَلَوْ عَلى أَنْفُسِكُمْ أَوِ الْوالِدَيْنِ وَالْأَقْرَبِينَ إِنْ يَكُنْ غَنِيًّا أَوْ فَقِيراً فَاللَّهُ أَوْلى بِهِما فَلا تَتَّبِعُوا الْهَوى أَنْ تَعْدِلُوا وَإِنْ تَلْوُوا أَوْ تُعْرِضُوا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيراً (135)","quran_en":"O believers, stand firmly for justice, bearing witness for God's sake even against yourselves, your parents or your nearest kin. Whether the person concerned is rich or poor, God has a greater claim over both. Do not follow desire, lest you depart from justice. If you distort your testimony or turn away, God is fully aware of what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>shuhadāʾ<\/em> may be a second predicate of “be” or a circumstantial expression. “God has a greater claim” supplies the reason: do not avoid testimony against the wealthy out of reverence or the poor out of pity, for God is more concerned for each than you are. The dual pronoun in “both” refers to the two classes of rich and poor, not simply to a singular person connected by “or,” which would require a singular pronoun. “That you act justly” is an expression of purpose, understood either as desire for justice or as aversion to deviating from the truth. The reading <em>talū<\/em>, with a <em>ḍamma<\/em> on the <em>lām<\/em>, is explained by transferring the <em>wāw<\/em>’s <em>ḍamma<\/em> to the <em>lām<\/em> and deleting one of the two <em>wāw<\/em>s; alternatively it derives from <em>wilāya<\/em>, exercising a governing charge.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: shuhadāʾ may be a second predicate of “be” or a circumstantial expression. “God has a greater claim” supplies the reason: do not avoid testimony against the wealthy out of reverence or the poor out of pity, for God is more concerned for each than you are. The dual pronoun in “both” refers to the two classes of rich and poor, not simply to a singular person connected by “or,” which would require a singular pronoun. “That you act justly” is an expression of purpose, understood either as desire for justice or as aversion to deviating from the truth. The reading talū , with a ḍamma on the lām , is explained by transferring the wāw ’s ḍamma to the lām and deleting one of the two wāw s; alternatively it derives from wilāya , exercising a governing charge."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>God commands persistence and strenuous commitment to equitable judgment. Witnesses are to give truthful testimony for God's pleasure, not for a gift or material return. This applies when giving the testimony is incumbent on that particular person and undertaking it causes no hardship; otherwise, compensation for his trouble is permitted. Even against themselves they must acknowledge debts or other facts, since testimony communicates the truth whether the fact favours the speaker or not. Filial respect and compassion must not prevent evidence against parents or relatives; still less should deference to strangers. Wealth must not awe the witness, and poverty must not lead to the suppression of truth through indulgent pity. God knows what benefits each and has instituted lawful testimony for their welfare. Do not follow passion by favouring either party. Twisting the tongue to deform testimony or turning away and suppressing it is known to God, who recompenses both the witness and the concealer.<\/p>\n<p>Ibn ʿAjība reports that, when this verse was revealed, the Prophet said: “Whoever believes in God and the Last Day, let him give his testimony against whomever it concerns. Whoever believes in God and the Last Day must not deny a right owed by him; let him discharge it willingly, without forcing the claimant to appeal to the ruler and contest the matter in order to recover his right. Any man who brings a case before me, and for whom I award a right against his brother that is not owed to him, must not take it: I am only allotting him a piece of Fire.” [Translator’s note: the final warning resembles the well-attested tradition concerning a judgment that awards another person’s right and thereby gives the recipient a piece of Fire; the combined wording as quoted warrants separate authentication.]<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God commands persistence and strenuous commitment to equitable judgment. Witnesses are to give truthful testimony for God's pleasure, not for a gift or material return. This applies when giving the testimony is incumbent on that particular person and undertaking it causes no hardship; otherwise, compensation for his trouble is permitted. Even against themselves they must acknowledge debts or other facts, since testimony communicates the truth whether the fact favours the speaker or not. Filial respect and compassion must not prevent evidence against parents or relatives; still less should deference to strangers. Wealth must not awe the witness, and poverty must not lead to the suppression of truth through indulgent pity. God knows what benefits each and has instituted lawful testimony for their welfare. Do not follow passion by favouring either party. Twisting the tongue to deform testimony or turning away and suppressing it is known to God, who recompenses both the witness and the concealer. Ibn ʿAjība reports that, when this verse was revealed, the Prophet said: “Whoever believes in God and the Last Day, let him give his testimony against whomever it concerns. Whoever believes in God and the Last Day must not deny a right owed by him; let him discharge it willingly, without forcing the claimant to appeal to the ruler and contest the matter in order to recover his right. Any man who brings a case before me, and for whom I award a right against his brother that is not owed to him, must not take it: I am only allotting him a piece of Fire.” [Translator’s note: the final warning resembles the well-attested tradition concerning a judgment that awards another person’s right and thereby gives the recipient a piece of Fire; the combined wording as quoted warrants separate authentication.]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Justice must be upheld in every matter, and concern for human approval must be abandoned. The seeker should watch the Real Sovereign rather than make people his audience, exercising firmness in truth against himself and others. Vigilance toward the Real does not coexist with vigilance toward created beings. Whoever watches the Real becomes absent from preoccupation with people; whoever watches people becomes absent from God and lives distressed by their opinions. The poet says:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The watcher of people's judgments dies of grief;<br>the courageous one wins the joys of life.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Ibn ʿAjība reports his grand-shaykh’s aphorism: “Vigilance toward created beings is a great matter for the people of the outward; absence of that vigilance is a great matter for the people of the inward.” Justice toward the self means not allowing it to incline toward concessions and interpretations; toward parents it means gentle remembrance and guidance to God; toward kin and strangers it means sincere advice for their true welfare, whether they are wealthy or poor. God gives success. With this treatment of the tongue complete, the next topic is safeguarding faith, the sixth theme contained in the sūrah.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Justice must be upheld in every matter, and concern for human approval must be abandoned. The seeker should watch the Real Sovereign rather than make people his audience, exercising firmness in truth against himself and others. Vigilance toward the Real does not coexist with vigilance toward created beings. Whoever watches the Real becomes absent from preoccupation with people; whoever watches people becomes absent from God and lives distressed by their opinions. The poet says: The watcher of people's judgments dies of grief; the courageous one wins the joys of life. Ibn ʿAjība reports his grand-shaykh’s aphorism: “Vigilance toward created beings is a great matter for the people of the outward; absence of that vigilance is a great matter for the people of the inward.” Justice toward the self means not allowing it to incline toward concessions and interpretations; toward parents it means gentle remembrance and guidance to God; toward kin and strangers it means sincere advice for their true welfare, whether they are wealthy or poor. God gives success. With this treatment of the tongue complete, the next topic is safeguarding faith, the sixth theme contained in the sūrah."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3766,"arabic_characters":3052},{"id":"q4_cont_136","q":"4:136","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:136 · Belief and revealed scriptures","source":"Cairo I:575","cairo":"I:575–576","source_page_first":575,"source_page_last":576,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا آمِنُوا بِاللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ وَالْكِتابِ الَّذِي نَزَّلَ عَلى رَسُولِهِ وَالْكِتابِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ مِنْ قَبْلُ وَمَنْ يَكْفُرْ بِاللَّهِ وَمَلائِكَتِهِ وَكُتُبِهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ فَقَدْ ضَلَّ ضَلالاً بَعِيداً (136)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: مخاطبًا من أسلم من اليهود- وهو عبد الله بن سَلاَم وأسَد وأسيد ابنا كَعبٍ، وثَعلبة بن قَيسٍ، وسلّام بن أخت عبد الله بن سلام، وسلمة ابن أخيه ويامين- قالوا يا رسول الله، نؤمن بك وبكتابك وبموسى والتوراة وعُزَّير، ونكفُرُ بما سِوَاهُ من الكُتب؟ فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «آمِنُوا بالله ورسوله محمد صلّى الله عليه وسلم وبكتابه القرآن، وبكل كتاب قبله» ، فَنزلت الآية.\nفقال لهم جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بمحمد، بعد أن آمنوا بموسى آمِنُوا بِاللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ وَالْكِتابِ الَّذِي نَزَّلَ عَلى رَسُولِهِ القرآن وَالْكِتابِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ مِنْ قَبْلُ أي: جنس الكتاب، فتدخل الكتب المتقدمة كلها، وَمَنْ يَكْفُرْ بِاللَّهِ وَمَلائِكَتِهِ وَكُتُبِهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ أي: ومن يكفر بشيء من ذلك فَقَدْ ضَلَّ ضَلالًا بَعِيداً أي: أخطأ خطًأ بعيدًا لا يكاد يعود إلى الطريق، فلما نزلت قالوا: يا رسول الله إنا نؤمن بالجميع، ولا نفرق بين أحد منهم، كما فرقت اليهود والنصارى.\nوقيل: الخطاب للمنافقين، أي: يا أيها الذين آمنوا بألسنتهم آمِنُوا بقلوبكم، كما آمنتم بألسنتكم، وقيل: للمؤمنين، أي: دُوموا على إيمانكم، وأثبتوا عليه.\nالإشارة: أمر الحق جل جلاله، أهل الإيمان أن يجددوا إيمانهم، فيثبتوا على ما هو حاصل، ويسترشدوا إلى ما ليس بحاصل، فإن أنوار الإيمان تتزايد وتترادف على القلوب بحسب التصفية والنظر، وبقدر الطاعة والتقرب، فلا يزال العبد يتقرب إلى الله، وأنوار التوجه تتوارد عليه، حتى تشرق عليه أنوار المواجهة وهي أنوار الشهود، فشروق الأنوار على قدر صفاء الأسرار، وورود الإمداد على حسب الاستعداد، فبقدر التفرغ من الأغيار ترد على\nالقلوب المواهب والأسرار، وهذا كله لمن صحب العارفين وأخذ عنهم، وملّك زمام نفسه لهم، وإلا فحَسبُه الإيمان بالغيب، ولو عمل ما عمل، وبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكر وعيد مَن ارتد عن الإيمان، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا آمِنُوا بِاللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ وَالْكِتابِ الَّذِي نَزَّلَ عَلى رَسُولِهِ وَالْكِتابِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ مِنْ قَبْلُ وَمَنْ يَكْفُرْ بِاللَّهِ وَمَلائِكَتِهِ وَكُتُبِهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ فَقَدْ ضَلَّ ضَلالاً بَعِيداً (136)","quran_en":"O you who believe, believe in God and His Messenger, the Book sent down to His Messenger, and the Book He sent down before. Whoever rejects God, His angels, His books, His messengers or the Last Day has wandered far astray.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Ibn ʿAjība reports an occasion involving certain Jews who embraced Islam, naming ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, Asad and Usayd sons of Kaʿb, Thaʿlaba ibn Qays, Sallām, the son of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām’s sister, Salama, the son of his brother, and Yāmīn. They professed belief in Muḥammad, the Qurʾān, Moses, Torah and Ezra, but asked whether they could reject the remaining revealed books. The Prophet, according to the account, commanded them to believe in God, Muḥammad, the Qurʾān and all scriptures revealed before it. The verse then instructed those who had accepted Muḥammad after Moses to affirm the entire genus of revealed scripture without dividing God's messengers into accepted and rejected. The report says they affirmed belief in all without distinction, unlike those Jews and Christians who divided the prophetic succession.<\/p>\n<p>Another explanation addresses hypocrites: you have professed belief with your tongues; now believe sincerely in your hearts. A further interpretation speaks to believers themselves: continue steadfastly in the faith already received. Whoever rejects any of these has gone so far astray that he scarcely returns to the path.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Ibn ʿAjība reports an occasion involving certain Jews who embraced Islam, naming ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, Asad and Usayd sons of Kaʿb, Thaʿlaba ibn Qays, Sallām, the son of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām’s sister, Salama, the son of his brother, and Yāmīn. They professed belief in Muḥammad, the Qurʾān, Moses, Torah and Ezra, but asked whether they could reject the remaining revealed books. The Prophet, according to the account, commanded them to believe in God, Muḥammad, the Qurʾān and all scriptures revealed before it. The verse then instructed those who had accepted Muḥammad after Moses to affirm the entire genus of revealed scripture without dividing God's messengers into accepted and rejected. The report says they affirmed belief in all without distinction, unlike those Jews and Christians who divided the prophetic succession. Another explanation addresses hypocrites: you have professed belief with your tongues; now believe sincerely in your hearts. A further interpretation speaks to believers themselves: continue steadfastly in the faith already received. Whoever rejects any of these has gone so far astray that he scarcely returns to the path."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>The call to believe commands the renewal and deepening of faith. Maintain what you already possess and seek instruction in what has not yet been realised. The lights of faith increase in succession upon hearts in proportion to their purification, contemplation, obedience and approach to God. As the servant draws nearer, the lights of <em>tawajjuh<\/em> (turning toward God) arrive until the lights of <em>muwājaha<\/em> (direct encounter), the lights of witnessing, shine forth. The appearance of lights corresponds to purification of inward secrets; spiritual aid (<em>imdād<\/em>) descends according to readiness (<em>istiʿdād<\/em>). As attention withdraws from what is other than God, gifts and mysteries are bestowed upon the heart. All of this belongs to one who accompanies the knowers, learns from them and places the reins of his soul in their hands; otherwise he must content himself with faith in the unseen, whatever deeds he performs. God gives success.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"The call to believe commands the renewal and deepening of faith. Maintain what you already possess and seek instruction in what has not yet been realised. The lights of faith increase in succession upon hearts in proportion to their purification, contemplation, obedience and approach to God. As the servant draws nearer, the lights of tawajjuh (turning toward God) arrive until the lights of muwājaha (direct encounter), the lights of witnessing, shine forth. The appearance of lights corresponds to purification of inward secrets; spiritual aid ( imdād ) descends according to readiness ( istiʿdād ). As attention withdraws from what is other than God, gifts and mysteries are bestowed upon the heart. All of this belongs to one who accompanies the knowers, learns from them and places the reins of his soul in their hands; otherwise he must content himself with faith in the unseen, whatever deeds he performs. God gives success."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2085,"arabic_characters":1907},{"id":"q4_cont_137","q":"4:137–139","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:137–139 · Repeated disbelief and hypocrisy","source":"Cairo I:576","cairo":"I:576–577","source_page_first":576,"source_page_last":577,"ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا ثُمَّ كَفَرُوا ثُمَّ آمَنُوا ثُمَّ كَفَرُوا ثُمَّ ازْدادُوا كُفْراً لَمْ يَكُنِ اللَّهُ لِيَغْفِرَ لَهُمْ وَلا لِيَهْدِيَهُمْ سَبِيلاً (137) بَشِّرِ الْمُنافِقِينَ بِأَنَّ لَهُمْ عَذاباً أَلِيماً (138) الَّذِينَ يَتَّخِذُونَ الْكافِرِينَ أَوْلِياءَ مِنْ دُونِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَيَبْتَغُونَ عِنْدَهُمُ الْعِزَّةَ فَإِنَّ الْعِزَّةَ لِلَّهِ جَمِيعاً (139)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا ثُمَّ كَفَرُوا ثم تكرر منهم الإيمان والكفر، ثم أصروا على الكفر وهم المنافقون، لَمْ يَكُنِ اللَّهُ لِيَغْفِرَ لَهُمْ لمَا سبق لهم من الشقاء، أو إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بموسى ثُمَّ كَفَرُوا بعبادة العجل ثُمَّ آمَنُوا حين تابوا ثُمَّ كَفَرُوا بعيسى ثُمَّ ازْدادُوا كُفْراً بمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلم، لَمْ يَكُنِ اللَّهُ لِيَغْفِرَ لَهُمْ، وهم اليهود، والأول أظهر لأن الكلام بعده في المنافقين، فقال تعالى في شأنهم: لَمْ يَكُنِ اللَّهُ لِيَغْفِرَ لَهُمْ وَلا لِيَهْدِيَهُمْ سَبِيلًا أي: طريقاً توصلهم إلى الحق، إذ يُستبعد منهم أن يتوبوا، فإن قلوبهم أشربت الكفر، وبصائرهم عميت، لا ينفع علاجها، لا أنهم لو أخلصوا الإيمان لم ينفعهم، وقد يكون إضلالهم عقابًا لسوء أفعالهم.\nثم ذكر وعيدهم فقال: بَشِّرِ الْمُنافِقِينَ بِأَنَّ لَهُمْ عَذاباً أَلِيماً. وهم الَّذِينَ يَتَّخِذُونَ الْكافِرِينَ أَوْلِياءَ أي: أحبابًا وأصدقاء مِنْ دُونِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ، وقد كان الكفار قبل ظهور الإسلام لهم الصولة والجاه، فطلب المنافقون أن ينالوا بولايتهم ومصادقتهم العزّ منهم، فردَّ الله عليهم بقوله: أَيَبْتَغُونَ عِنْدَهُمُ الْعِزَّةَ بولايتهم؟ فَإِنَّ الْعِزَّةَ لِلَّهِ جَمِيعاً ولرسوله ولأوليائه، ولا عزة لغيره إذ لا يعبأ بعزة لا تدوم ويعقبها الذل.\nالإشارة: من كان ضعيفَ الاعتقاد في أهل الخصوصية، ضعيفَ التصديق، تراه تارة يدخل وتارة يخرج، وتارة يصدق وتارة ينكر، لا يُرجى فلاحُه في طريق الخصوص، فإن ضم إلى ذلك صحبة أهل الإنكار وولايتهم، فبشره بالخيبة والخسران، فإن تعزز بعزهم أعقبه الذل والهوان، والعياذ بالله من الخذلان، فالعز إنما يكون بعز التوحيد والإيمان، وعزة المعرفة والإحسان، وبصحبة أهل العرفان، الذين تعززوا بعز الرحمن، فمن تعزز بعز يفنى مات عزه، ومن تعزز بعز يبقى دام عزه، والشبكة التي يصطاد بها العزّ هو الذل لله، يظهره بين عباد الله. قال بعضهم: والله ما رأيتُ العزّ إلاَّ في الذل. وقال الشاعر:\nتذلَّلْ لمَن تَهْوَى لِتَكْسِبَ عِزةً ... فكم عزةٍ قد نالها المرء بالذّلّ\nوبالله التوفيق.\nثم نهى عن صحبة أهل الخوض، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا ثُمَّ كَفَرُوا ثُمَّ آمَنُوا ثُمَّ كَفَرُوا ثُمَّ ازْدادُوا كُفْراً لَمْ يَكُنِ اللَّهُ لِيَغْفِرَ لَهُمْ وَلا لِيَهْدِيَهُمْ سَبِيلاً (137) بَشِّرِ الْمُنافِقِينَ بِأَنَّ لَهُمْ عَذاباً أَلِيماً (138) الَّذِينَ يَتَّخِذُونَ الْكافِرِينَ أَوْلِياءَ مِنْ دُونِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَيَبْتَغُونَ عِنْدَهُمُ الْعِزَّةَ فَإِنَّ الْعِزَّةَ لِلَّهِ جَمِيعاً (139)","quran_en":"Those who believed, then disbelieved, then believed again, then disbelieved and increased in disbelief—God will not forgive them or guide them to a path. Announce to the hypocrites the painful punishment awaiting them: those who take unbelievers as close allies instead of believers. Do they seek dignity with them? All dignity belongs to God.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>One reading concerns hypocrites who alternate profession of faith and unbelief, eventually persisting in denial. God will not forgive them because wretchedness has already been allotted to them. Another interpretation concerns Jews: they believed in Moses, disbelieved through worship of the calf, repented and believed again, rejected Jesus, and increased unbelief by rejecting Muḥammad. Ibn ʿAjība prefers the first in view of the verses about hypocrisy that follow. The denial of forgiveness and guidance is explained by how unlikely their repentance is: their hearts are imbued with unbelief and their inner sight is blind, beyond treatment. It does not mean that sincere faith, were they to embrace it, would not benefit them. God’s leading them astray may also be a punishment for their evil deeds.<\/p>\n<p>The hypocrites sought intimate protection from unbelievers rather than believers because, before the visible rise of Islam, those unbelievers held power and prestige. The hope was to acquire dignity by association. God rebukes such dependence: all lasting dignity belongs to Him, His Messenger and those attached to Him. Dignity that ends in humiliation is unworthy of the name.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"One reading concerns hypocrites who alternate profession of faith and unbelief, eventually persisting in denial. God will not forgive them because wretchedness has already been allotted to them. Another interpretation concerns Jews: they believed in Moses, disbelieved through worship of the calf, repented and believed again, rejected Jesus, and increased unbelief by rejecting Muḥammad. Ibn ʿAjība prefers the first in view of the verses about hypocrisy that follow. The denial of forgiveness and guidance is explained by how unlikely their repentance is: their hearts are imbued with unbelief and their inner sight is blind, beyond treatment. It does not mean that sincere faith, were they to embrace it, would not benefit them. God’s leading them astray may also be a punishment for their evil deeds. The hypocrites sought intimate protection from unbelievers rather than believers because, before the visible rise of Islam, those unbelievers held power and prestige. The hope was to acquire dignity by association. God rebukes such dependence: all lasting dignity belongs to Him, His Messenger and those attached to Him. Dignity that ends in humiliation is unworthy of the name."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>One whose confidence in the people of special spiritual knowledge is weak alternates between entering their company and leaving, believing them one moment and denying them the next. Success for such a person in the special path is not to be hoped for. Joining those who reject its people makes the failure worse. If a person borrows prestige from opponents of the friends of God, abasement follows. Dignity comes from divine unity, faith, inward recognition and excellence, and companionship of knowers honoured by the Merciful. Borrowed dignity dies when its source perishes; divine dignity endures. The net with which honour is caught is humility before God, displayed among God’s servants. One person said, “By God, I have only seen honour in abasement.” A poet says:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Humble yourself before the one you love and acquire honour;<br>how much dignity has one obtained by humble surrender!<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n","authorial":true,"text":"One whose confidence in the people of special spiritual knowledge is weak alternates between entering their company and leaving, believing them one moment and denying them the next. Success for such a person in the special path is not to be hoped for. Joining those who reject its people makes the failure worse. If a person borrows prestige from opponents of the friends of God, abasement follows. Dignity comes from divine unity, faith, inward recognition and excellence, and companionship of knowers honoured by the Merciful. Borrowed dignity dies when its source perishes; divine dignity endures. The net with which honour is caught is humility before God, displayed among God’s servants. One person said, “By God, I have only seen honour in abasement.” A poet says: Humble yourself before the one you love and acquire honour; how much dignity has one obtained by humble surrender!"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2068,"arabic_characters":2218},{"id":"q4_cont_140","q":"4:140–141","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:140–141 · The company one keeps","source":"Cairo I:577–578","cairo":"I:577–578","source_page_first":577,"source_page_last":578,"ar":"وَقَدْ نَزَّلَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِي الْكِتابِ أَنْ إِذا سَمِعْتُمْ آياتِ اللَّهِ يُكْفَرُ بِها وَيُسْتَهْزَأُ بِها فَلا تَقْعُدُوا مَعَهُمْ حَتَّى يَخُوضُوا فِي حَدِيثٍ غَيْرِهِ إِنَّكُمْ إِذاً مِثْلُهُمْ ...\nقلت: (أن) مخففة: نافية، فاعل نزّل، و (يكفر) و (يُستهزأ) ، حالان من الآيات، وضمير (معهم) : يعود على الكفار المفهوم من (يكفر) ، وضمير (غيره) يعود على الكفر والاستهزاء، وهما شيء واحد.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في التحذير من مجالسة أهل الكفر والمعاصي: وَقَدْ نَزَّلَ عَلَيْكُمْ يا معشر المسلمين في القرآن في سورة الأنعام، أنه إِذا سَمِعْتُمْ آياتِ اللَّهِ حال كونها يُكْفَرُ بِها، وَيُسْتَهْزَأُ بِها، فَلا تَقْعُدُوا مَعَهُمْ بل قوموا عنهم، إن لم تقدروا أن تنكروا عليهم، والآية التي في سورة الأنعام قوله تعالى:\nوَإِذا رَأَيْتَ الَّذِينَ يَخُوضُونَ فِي آياتِنا فَأَعْرِضْ عَنْهُمْ حَتَّى يَخُوضُوا فِي حَدِيثٍ غَيْرِهِ الآية. فما داموا في الخوض فاعرضوا عنهم حتى يخوضوا في حديث غير الخوض، فإن جلستم معهم في حال الخوض فإنكم إِذاً مِثْلُهُمْ في الإثم، إن لم ترضوا، أو في الكفر، إن رضيتم بخوضهم.\nنزلت في قوم من المنافقين كانوا يجلسون إلى أحبار اليهود، فيسخرون من القرآن، ويكذبون به ويحرفونه، فنهى المسلمين عن مجالستهم، قال ابن عباس: ودخل في هذه الآية كُلُّ مُحدِث في الدين ومُبتدعٍ إلى يوم القيامة. هـ.\nالإشارة: أولياء الله آيات من آيات الله: فمن استهزأ بهم فقد استوجب المقت من الله، وكل موطن يقع فيه الإنكار عليهم أو الغض من مرتبتهم، يجب الفرار منه، لأنه موطن الغضب ومحل الهلاك والعَطب، فإن لحوم الأولياء سموم قاتلة، واللعنة على من يقع فيهم حاصلة، فمن جلس مع أهل الخوض من غير عذر، كان من الخائضين، ومن فرّ منهم كان من الناجين، ومن أنكر على من يقع فيهم كان من المجاهدين. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر وعيد الخائضين ومن رضي بخوضهم، فقال:\n... إِنَّ اللَّهَ جامِعُ الْمُنافِقِينَ وَالْكافِرِينَ فِي جَهَنَّمَ جَمِيعاً (140) الَّذِينَ يَتَرَبَّصُونَ بِكُمْ فَإِنْ كانَ لَكُمْ فَتْحٌ مِنَ اللَّهِ قالُوا أَلَمْ نَكُنْ مَعَكُمْ وَإِنْ كانَ لِلْكافِرِينَ نَصِيبٌ قالُوا أَلَمْ نَسْتَحْوِذْ عَلَيْكُمْ وَنَمْنَعْكُمْ مِنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ فَاللَّهُ يَحْكُمُ بَيْنَكُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ وَلَنْ يَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ لِلْكافِرِينَ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ سَبِيلاً (141)\nقلت: (الذين) : صفة المنافقين، أو نصب على الذم، و (نستحوذ) : نغلب، استحوذ: غلب، جاء على أصله، ولم يُعلّ كاستعاذ والقياس: استحاذ، يستحيذ، كاستعاذ يستعيذ، لكنه صحح تنبيهًا على الأصل.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ اللَّهَ سيجمع الْمُنافِقِينَ وَالْكافِرِينَ، أي: الخائضين والقاعدين معهم، فِي جَهَنَّمَ جَمِيعاً خالدين فيها. الَّذِينَ يَتَرَبَّصُونَ بِكُمْ أي: ينتظرون بكم الدوائر، أي: ما يدور به الزمان والدهر عليكم، وهم المنافقون، فَإِنْ كانَ لَكُمْ فَتْحٌ مِنَ اللَّهِ كالنصر والغنيمة قالُوا للمؤمنين: أَلَمْ نَكُنْ مَعَكُمْ على دينكم، فأعطونا مما غنمتم، وَإِنْ كانَ لِلْكافِرِينَ نَصِيبٌ دولة أو ظهور على المسلمين، قالُوا لهم: أَلَمْ نَسْتَحْوِذْ عَلَيْكُمْ أي: نغلبكم ونتمكن من قتلكم، وأبقينا عليكم فمنعناكم من قتل المسلمين لكم، بأن خذلناهم بتخييل ما ضعُفت به عزيمتهم عليكم، وتوانينا في مظاهرتهم عليكم، فأشركُونا مما أصبتم. وإنما سمي ظفر المسلمين فتحًا، وظفر الكافرين نصيبًا لخسة حظه، فإنه حظ دنياوي، استدراجًا ومكرًا، بخلاف ظفر المسلمين، فإنه إظهار الدين، وإعانة بالغنيمة للمسلمين.\nفَاللَّهُ يَحْكُمُ بَيْنَهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ فيدخل أهل الحق الجنة، ويدخل أهل الخوض النار، وَلَنْ يَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ لِلْكافِرِينَ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ سَبِيلًا أي: حجة، أو غلبة في الدنيا والآخرة، وفيه دليل على عدم صحة ملك الكافر للمسلم، فيباع عليه إن اشتراه، ويفسخ نكاحه إن تزوج مسلمة. والله تعالى أعلم الإشارة: (المرء مع مَن أحب) من أحب قوما حشر معهم، فمن أحب أهل الخوض حُشر مع الخائضين، ومن أحب أهل الصفا حشر مع المخلصين، وإن كان مذبذبًا يميل مع كل ريح حشر مع المخلطين، وهو من خف عقله وضعف يقينه، إن رأى بأهل النسبة من الفقراء عزًا ونصرًا وفتحًا انحاز إليهم، وقال: ألم نكن معكم، وإن رأى لأهل الإنكار من العوام صولة وغلبة رجع إليهم، وقال: ألم نستحوذ عليكم ونمنعكم من دعاء الصالحين عليكم، فما لهذا عند الله من خلاق. وفي الحديث: «ذو الوجهين لا يكون عند الله وجيهًا» . فالله يحكم بينهم يوم القيامة، فيرفع أهل الصفا مع المقربين، ويسقط أهل الخوض مع الخائضين، وليس لأهل الخوض من أهل الإنكار سبيل ولا حجة على أهل الصفا من الأبرار، إِنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الَّذِينَ اتَّقَوْا وَالَّذِينَ هُمْ مُحْسِنُونَ.\nثم ذكر أحوالهم الشنيعة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقَدْ نَزَّلَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِي الْكِتابِ أَنْ إِذا سَمِعْتُمْ آياتِ اللَّهِ يُكْفَرُ بِها وَيُسْتَهْزَأُ بِها فَلا تَقْعُدُوا مَعَهُمْ حَتَّى يَخُوضُوا فِي حَدِيثٍ غَيْرِهِ إِنَّكُمْ إِذاً مِثْلُهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ جامِعُ الْمُنافِقِينَ وَالْكافِرِينَ فِي جَهَنَّمَ جَمِيعاً (140) الَّذِينَ يَتَرَبَّصُونَ بِكُمْ فَإِنْ كانَ لَكُمْ فَتْحٌ مِنَ اللَّهِ قالُوا أَلَمْ نَكُنْ مَعَكُمْ وَإِنْ كانَ لِلْكافِرِينَ نَصِيبٌ قالُوا أَلَمْ نَسْتَحْوِذْ عَلَيْكُمْ وَنَمْنَعْكُمْ مِنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ فَاللَّهُ يَحْكُمُ بَيْنَكُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ وَلَنْ يَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ لِلْكافِرِينَ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ سَبِيلاً (141)","quran_en":"He has already revealed to you in the Book that, when you hear God's verses denied or mocked, you must not sit with those doing so until they turn to another conversation, or else you become like them. God will gather hypocrites and unbelievers together in Hell. They wait to see what befalls you: when you gain a victory from God they say, “Were we not with you?” But when unbelievers gain an advantage they say, “Did we not gain the upper hand over you and protect you against the believers?” God will judge between you on the Day of Resurrection, and He will not grant unbelievers a way against believers.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>an<\/em> is “lightened”; Cairo then prints the difficult words <span lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">نافية، فاعل نزل<\/span>, literally “negative; the subject of <span lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">نزل<\/span>.” [Translator’s textual note: The heading reads active <em>nazzala<\/em>, “He has revealed,” while the gloss is inconsistent as printed. Al-Bayḍāwī on 4:140 explicitly distinguishes ʿĀṣim’s active reading from the others’ passive reading, calls <em>an<\/em> lightened, and makes its clause stand as the passive subject. That documented analysis explains the proposed conjecture <span lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">نائبة فاعل<\/span>, “standing as the passive subject.” It does not establish that these were Ibn ʿAjība’s own words: Cairo prints neither that phrase nor a passive verb in the heading. Both difficulties remain disclosed; the text is not recast as an analysis of explanatory <em>an<\/em>. See source check XII-S063.] The verbal expressions “denied” and “mocked” describe the circumstances of the divine verses. The pronoun “them” refers to those who deny, implied by the passive verb; “other than it” refers to the combined activity of disbelief and mockery. Later “those who wait” is an adjective describing hypocrites or is accusative as an expression of censure. <em>Nastaḥwidh<\/em> means “we gain mastery,” retaining an older verbal form whose consonantal pattern did not undergo the expected phonological alteration, as <em>istaʿādha<\/em> normally does.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: an is “lightened”; Cairo then prints the difficult words نافية، فاعل نزل , literally “negative; the subject of نزل .” [Translator’s textual note: The heading reads active nazzala , “He has revealed,” while the gloss is inconsistent as printed. Al-Bayḍāwī on 4:140 explicitly distinguishes ʿĀṣim’s active reading from the others’ passive reading, calls an lightened, and makes its clause stand as the passive subject. That documented analysis explains the proposed conjecture نائبة فاعل , “standing as the passive subject.” It does not establish that these were Ibn ʿAjība’s own words: Cairo prints neither that phrase nor a passive verb in the heading. Both difficulties remain disclosed; the text is not recast as an analysis of explanatory an . See source check XII-S063.] The verbal expressions “denied” and “mocked” describe the circumstances of the divine verses. The pronoun “them” refers to those who deny, implied by the passive verb; “other than it” refers to the combined activity of disbelief and mockery. Later “those who wait” is an adjective describing hypocrites or is accusative as an expression of censure. Nastaḥwidh means “we gain mastery,” retaining an older verbal form whose consonantal pattern did not undergo the expected phonological alteration, as istaʿādha normally does."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Muslims had already been instructed in al-Anʿām (6:68) to turn aside from people indulging in the derision of divine signs. If unable to denounce it, they should leave the conversation until it changes. If you sit with them while they engage in that talk, you are like them in sin if you do not approve of it, or in unbelief if you do approve of it. The verse is said to concern hypocrites who sat with Jewish scholars mocking and distorting the Qurʾān. Ibn ʿAbbās says that this verse includes everyone who introduces a novelty in religion and every innovator until the Day of Resurrection.<\/p>\n<p>God will gather those who engage in derisive talk and those who sit with them together in Hell, where they will abide. Hypocrites, meanwhile, wait for reversals in Muslim fortunes. If believers gain victory and spoils, they demand a share on the ground of their professed membership. If opponents gain ground, they claim to have spared them, weakened the Muslims' resolve through deception, and were slow to support the believers against them, and demand reward from the other side. The text calls Muslim success a <em>fatḥ<\/em>, an opening, but unbelievers' momentary success only a <em>naṣīb<\/em>, a worldly portion: Ibn ʿAjība treats the former as the strengthening of true religion and the latter as a paltry worldly share, involving gradual enticement to ruin and a stratagem. Ultimately God decides the dispute, admitting the people of truth into the Garden and the deliberate mockers into punishment. He interprets “no way” as no proof or dominance in this world or the next and additionally deduces historical juristic rules forbidding non-Muslim ownership of a Muslim slave and requiring the sale of such a slave if acquired, as well as dissolving his marriage if he marries a Muslim woman. [Translator’s note: these are juristic deductions recorded in this historical commentary.]<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Muslims had already been instructed in al-Anʿām (6:68) to turn aside from people indulging in the derision of divine signs. If unable to denounce it, they should leave the conversation until it changes. If you sit with them while they engage in that talk, you are like them in sin if you do not approve of it, or in unbelief if you do approve of it. The verse is said to concern hypocrites who sat with Jewish scholars mocking and distorting the Qurʾān. Ibn ʿAbbās says that this verse includes everyone who introduces a novelty in religion and every innovator until the Day of Resurrection. God will gather those who engage in derisive talk and those who sit with them together in Hell, where they will abide. Hypocrites, meanwhile, wait for reversals in Muslim fortunes. If believers gain victory and spoils, they demand a share on the ground of their professed membership. If opponents gain ground, they claim to have spared them, weakened the Muslims' resolve through deception, and were slow to support the believers against them, and demand reward from the other side. The text calls Muslim success a fatḥ , an opening, but unbelievers' momentary success only a naṣīb , a worldly portion: Ibn ʿAjība treats the former as the strengthening of true religion and the latter as a paltry worldly share, involving gradual enticement to ruin and a stratagem. Ultimately God decides the dispute, admitting the people of truth into the Garden and the deliberate mockers into punishment. He interprets “no way” as no proof or dominance in this world or the next and additionally deduces historical juristic rules forbidding non-Muslim ownership of a Muslim slave and requiring the sale of such a slave if acquired, as well as dissolving his marriage if he marries a Muslim woman. [Translator’s note: these are juristic deductions recorded in this historical commentary.]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>God's saints are among His signs. In Ibn ʿAjība’s spiritual application, anyone who derides them has made himself deserving of God’s abhorrence. It is obligatory to flee any place where they are repudiated or their rank is disparaged, for it is a place of wrath, destruction and ruin. He says, “The flesh of the saints is a deadly poison, and the curse falls upon those who disparage them.” Remaining without excuse makes one a participant; departing protects one; speaking against the slander is a kind of struggle. “A person is with those whom he loves”: love of contentious mockers joins them, love of the pure joins the sincere, and opportunistic vacillation attaches one to confused companions. Such a weakly grounded person joins Sufi poor (<em>fuqarāʾ<\/em>) when their recognition brings influence and falls back among their deniers when the latter hold power. Such a person has no share with God. The hadith says, “A two-faced man is not esteemed before God.” God raises the people of purity with those brought near, and brings the mockers to the consequences of their acts. Those denying the people of purity have no genuine argument against them. “God is with those who are reverently mindful and excel” (16:128).<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"God's saints are among His signs. In Ibn ʿAjība’s spiritual application, anyone who derides them has made himself deserving of God’s abhorrence. It is obligatory to flee any place where they are repudiated or their rank is disparaged, for it is a place of wrath, destruction and ruin. He says, “The flesh of the saints is a deadly poison, and the curse falls upon those who disparage them.” Remaining without excuse makes one a participant; departing protects one; speaking against the slander is a kind of struggle. “A person is with those whom he loves”: love of contentious mockers joins them, love of the pure joins the sincere, and opportunistic vacillation attaches one to confused companions. Such a weakly grounded person joins Sufi poor ( fuqarāʾ ) when their recognition brings influence and falls back among their deniers when the latter hold power. Such a person has no share with God. The hadith says, “A two-faced man is not esteemed before God.” God raises the people of purity with those brought near, and brings the mockers to the consequences of their acts. Those denying the people of purity have no genuine argument against them. “God is with those who are reverently mindful and excel” (16:128)."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4387,"arabic_characters":4041}];
